## What Is The RS Score

The Replication Stress (RS) score is a **binary genomic alteration flag**, NOT a transcriptomic signature. It is derived from standard NGS copy number + mutation calls — no RNA-seq, no RAD51 foci, no specialized assay needed.

### RS-High triggers (≥1 = RS-High)

| Category | Alteration | Vector value |
|---|---|---|
| RB pathway | CCNE1 copy number > 4 | rss = 0.80 |
| RB pathway | RB1 loss (deletion or LoF mutation) | rss = 0.80 |
| RB pathway | CDKN2A deletion | rss = 0.80 |
| RB pathway | CDKN2B deletion | rss = 0.80 |
| Oncogene-induced RS | MYC amplification | rss = 0.80 |
| Oncogene-induced RS | MYCL1 amplification | rss = 0.80 |
| Oncogene-induced RS | KRAS amplification (copy number, not point mut) | rss = 0.80 |
| Oncogene-induced RS | ERBB2/HER2 amplification | rss = 0.80 |
| Oncogene-induced RS | NF1 loss-of-function or deletion | rss = 0.80 |

**RS-Low:** None of the above present → rss = 0.20

**Clinical meaning:**
- **RS-High:** Tumor already has sufficient replication stress → gemcitabine alone is sufficient → adding ATRi (berzosertib) provides NO benefit (HR 1.11 in the trial)
- **RS-Low:** Tumor under the lethality threshold → gemcitabine + ATRi tips it over → benefit (HR 0.34 in the trial)

